/**
 * @page c_to_cpp_guide C and C++ API comparison
 *
 * libeasymcp2221++ is a C++17 adapter around
 * <a href="https://github.com/tobiasvogel/libeasymcp2221">libeasymcp2221</a>.
 * The C library still performs USB transport and MCP2221 protocol handling;
 * the C++ layer changes how applications express ownership, errors, state,
 * and configuration.
 *
 * This page is organized by task rather than by class. It is intended both
 * for users migrating existing C code and for users who want to understand
 * how the C++ API maps onto the underlying C API.
 *
 * @section ccpp_summary Quick mapping
 *
 * | C API concept | C++ API counterpart | Main difference |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `mcp2221_t *` | @ref libeasymcp2221::Device "Device" | RAII lifetime; move-only root object |
 * | `mcp2221_error_code_t` | @ref libeasymcp2221::Error "Error" / `ErrorCode` | Exceptions plus preserved native error code |
 * | `mcp2221_close()` | `Device` destructor or `Device::close()` | Automatic cleanup by default |
 * | `mcp2221_i2c_slave_t` | @ref libeasymcp2221::I2cDevice "I2cDevice" | Stateful target object with typed options |
 * | SMBus helper functions | @ref libeasymcp2221::SmbusDevice "SmbusDevice" | Target address retained by the object |
 * | GPIO read/write structs | @ref libeasymcp2221::GpioState "GpioState" / @ref libeasymcp2221::GpioWrite "GpioWrite" | `std::optional` represents unavailable/preserved pins |
 * | GPIO poll state + filter mask | @ref libeasymcp2221::GpioPoller "GpioPoller" / @ref libeasymcp2221::GpioEventFilter "GpioEventFilter" | Poll history and filter owned by an object |
 * | string/magic-value configuration | strongly typed enums | Compile-time type checking |
 * | raw flash-info output struct | @ref libeasymcp2221::FlashInfo "FlashInfo" | Raw data plus decoded `std::string` fields |
 *
 * @section ccpp_open Opening and closing a device
 *
 * In C, the caller owns the handle and must explicitly close it:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * mcp2221_t *dev = NULL;
 * mcp2221_error_code_t err =
 *     mcp2221_open_simple(0x04D8, 0x00DD, 0, NULL, 100000, &dev);
 *
 * if (err != MCP2221_ERR_OK)
 *     return 1;
 *
 * // use dev
 * mcp2221_close(dev);
 * @endcode
 *
 * In C++, construction opens the device and destruction releases the root
 * object's reference automatically:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * using namespace libeasymcp2221;
 *
 * Device device;
 * // use device
 * @endcode
 *
 * `Device::close()` is available when explicit early release is useful.
 *
 * @section ccpp_errors Error handling
 *
 * C code normally checks every returned `mcp2221_error_code_t`:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * mcp2221_error_code_t err = mcp2221_i2c_set_speed(dev, 100000);
 * if (err != MCP2221_ERR_OK) {
 *     fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", mcp2221_error_code_to_string(err));
 *     return 1;
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * The C++ adapter translates failures to @ref libeasymcp2221::Error "Error":
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * try {
 *     Device device;
 *     device.setI2cSpeed(100000);
 * }
 * catch (const Error& error) {
 *     std::cerr << error.what() << '\n';
 *     std::cerr << "typed code: " << static_cast<int>(error.code()) << '\n';
 *     std::cerr << "native C code: " << error.nativeCode() << '\n';
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * `Error::nativeCode()` preserves the original C error value for applications
 * that need to interoperate with existing diagnostics or policy.
 *
 * @section ccpp_i2c Raw I2C access
 *
 * Bus-wide operations remain methods on @ref libeasymcp2221::Device "Device":
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * Device device;
 * device.setI2cSpeed(100000);
 *
 * device.i2cWrite(0x50, std::vector<std::uint8_t>{0x00, 0x10});
 * const auto data = device.i2cRead(0x50, 16);
 * @endcode
 *
 * For repeated access to one target, prefer @ref libeasymcp2221::I2cDevice
 * "I2cDevice".
 *
 * @section ccpp_i2c_device Stateful I2C targets
 *
 * The C API stores target settings in `mcp2221_i2c_slave_t`:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * mcp2221_i2c_slave_t ee;
 * mcp2221_i2c_slave_init(
 *     &ee, dev, 0x50, 1, 100000, 2, MCP2221_I2C_BYTE_ORDER_BIG);
 *
 * uint8_t data[16];
 * mcp2221_i2c_slave_read_register(
 *     &ee, 0x0000, data, sizeof(data), 0,
 *     MCP2221_I2C_BYTE_ORDER_DEFAULT);
 * @endcode
 *
 * The C++ counterpart expresses the same configuration through typed options:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * I2cDeviceOptions options{};
 * options.force = true;
 * options.speedHz = 100000;
 * options.registerWidth = RegisterWidth::Bits16;
 * options.byteOrder = ByteOrder::BigEndian;
 *
 * auto eeprom = device.i2cDevice(0x50, options);
 * const auto data = eeprom.readRegister(0x0000, 16);
 * @endcode
 *
 * See the parallel examples:
 * - C: `examples/test_i2c.c` in libeasymcp2221 v2.0.2
 * - C++: `examples/test_i2c.cpp`
 *
 * The same object-oriented pattern is used by `scan_i2c.cpp` and
 * `ssd1306_i2c.cpp`.
 *
 * @section ccpp_smbus SMBus
 *
 * Instead of passing a device handle and target information into each SMBus
 * helper, create a stateful target:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * auto sensor = device.smbusDevice(0x5A);
 *
 * const auto status = sensor.readByteData(0x01);
 * const auto word = sensor.readWordData(0x02);
 * sensor.writeByteData(0x03, 0x7F);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @ref libeasymcp2221::SmbusDevice "SmbusDevice" also exposes process calls,
 * SMBus block operations, and fixed-length I2C-style block operations.
 *
 * @section ccpp_gpio GPIO configuration and I/O
 *
 * C uses explicit pin-function and GPIO read/write structures. The C++ layer
 * uses typed pin identifiers, typed functions, and `std::optional` for partial
 * updates.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * PinConfigurations configuration{};
 * configuration[0] = {PinFunction::GpioOutput, true};
 * configuration[1] = {PinFunction::GpioInput, false};
 * device.configurePins(configuration);
 *
 * GpioWrite write{};
 * write.pins[0] = false;
 * device.writeGpio(write);
 *
 * const auto state = device.readGpio();
 * @endcode
 *
 * A disengaged `GpioWrite::pins` entry means "preserve this output". A
 * disengaged `GpioState::pins` entry means the pin is currently not configured
 * as GPIO.
 *
 * Parallel examples:
 * - `gpio_basic.c` / `gpio_basic.cpp`
 * - `set_pin_functions.c` / `set_pin_functions.cpp`
 *
 * @section ccpp_gpio_poll GPIO edge polling
 *
 * The C API requires a caller-owned poll state and a bit mask. The C++ API
 * packages both into @ref libeasymcp2221::GpioPoller "GpioPoller":
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * device.setPinFunction(Pin::GP0, PinFunction::GpioInput);
 *
 * auto poller = device.gpioPoller();
 * GpioEventFilter filter{};
 * filter.rising[0] = true;
 * poller.setFilter(filter);
 *
 * for (;;) {
 *     const auto events = poller.pollEvents(8);
 *     if (!events.empty())
 *         break;
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * See `gpio_poll_events.c` / `gpio_poll_events.cpp`.
 *
 * @section ccpp_analog ADC, DAC, and clock output
 *
 * C configuration strings and numeric selectors become strongly typed enum
 * values:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * device.configureAdc(VoltageReference::Internal1V024);
 * const auto adc = device.readAdcRaw();
 *
 * device.configureDac(VoltageReference::Internal2V048, 16);
 * device.writeDacRaw(8);
 *
 * device.configureClock(ClockDutyCycle::Percent50, ClockFrequency::MHz12);
 * @endcode
 *
 * This is the direct C++ counterpart of `analog_demo.c`; see
 * `analog_demo.cpp`.
 *
 * @section ccpp_sram SRAM configuration
 *
 * @ref libeasymcp2221::SramConfig "SramConfig" is a partial update type.
 * Every disengaged field preserves the corresponding runtime SRAM setting:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * SramConfig config{};
 * config.gpio[0].function = SramPinFunction::Gpio;
 * config.gpio[0].direction = GpioDirection::Output;
 * config.gpio[0].outputValue = true;
 * config.clock.frequency = ClockFrequency::MHz12;
 *
 * device.configureSram(config);
 * @endcode
 *
 * This changes runtime SRAM only; it does not write flash.
 *
 * @section ccpp_flash Flash information and persistent configuration
 *
 * Aggregate flash information is returned as @ref libeasymcp2221::FlashInfo
 * "FlashInfo":
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * const auto info = device.flashInfo();
 * std::cout << info.usbManufacturer << '\n';
 * std::cout << info.usbProduct << '\n';
 * std::cout << info.usbSerial << '\n';
 * std::cout << info.factorySerial << '\n';
 * @endcode
 *
 * Persistent configuration writes remain explicit:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * device.saveConfigurationToFlash();
 * @endcode
 *
 * @warning `saveConfigurationToFlash()` performs persistent writes and is not
 * atomic. Use it only when persistence is intended.
 *
 * See `flash_info_save.c` / `flash_info_save.cpp`.
 *
 * @section ccpp_usb USB enumeration attributes
 *
 * USB settings are first staged and then optionally persisted:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * device.stageUsbRemoteWakeup(true);
 * device.stageUsbSelfPowered(false);
 * device.stageUsbRequestedCurrent(100);
 *
 * device.saveConfigurationToFlash();
 * @endcode
 *
 * The host sees persisted USB enumeration changes only after re-enumeration.
 * See `usb_power.c` / `usb_power.cpp`.
 *
 * @section ccpp_examples Parallel example index
 *
 * The following C++ examples intentionally mirror examples from
 * libeasymcp2221 v2.0.2:
 *
 * | C example | C++ counterpart | Demonstrates |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `analog_demo.c` | `analog_demo.cpp` | ADC, DAC, clock |
 * | `flash_info_save.c` | `flash_info_save.cpp` | Flash info and persistent save |
 * | `gpio_basic.c` | `gpio_basic.cpp` | GPIO configuration/read/write |
 * | `gpio_poll_events.c` | `gpio_poll_events.cpp` | Filtered edge polling |
 * | `scan_i2c.c` | `scan_i2c.cpp` | I2C bus scan |
 * | `set_pin_functions.c` | `set_pin_functions.cpp` | Pin-function mapping |
 * | `ssd1306_i2c.c` | `ssd1306_i2c.cpp` | Stateful I2C peripheral use |
 * | `test_i2c.c` | `test_i2c.cpp` | Register-oriented EEPROM access |
 * | `usb_power.c` | `usb_power.cpp` | USB enumeration attributes |
 *
 * The C++ repository also contains smaller API-oriented examples such as
 * `basic.cpp`, `i2c_device.cpp`, `smbus_device.cpp`, `gpio.cpp`, `analog.cpp`,
 * `sram.cpp`, and `flash_usb.cpp`.
 */
